nagorno-karabakh may be small in size  but the disputed region is rich in  cultural and religious heritage  this hundred year old monuments however  have seen better days  hundreds of monuments and historical  sites in this region have been damaged  vandalized or destroyed during the 30  years of war  thousands of graveyards the historical  educational institutions  museums they're now unrecognizable the  first publication house which was  established in the 19th century is  ruined  the russian orthodox church hajjal akbar  mosque the church of albania  merdingli mosque and murali shrine from  the 13th century are also damaged  in the immediate aftermath of the  ceasefire that ended last year's war  between azerbaijan and armenia  cultural institutions and scholars  voiced concerns  they said hundreds of historical mosques  church  monasteries monuments and museums could  be at risk  armenia says between 1997 and 2006  azerbaijani forces damaged or destroyed  thousands of the historical sites  including medieval church and graveyards  in narc japan but azerbaijan says the  cultural damage on his side  became more apparent after its armed  forces recaptured territories  armenia had controlled since 1993  and that the scale of the destruction to  sites including mosques and libraries  is bigger than expected experts believe  cultural reconciliation is still  possible in nagorno-karabakh  despite the dismal record of the past  three decades both sides have  demonstrated awareness  and even admiration of heritage that  isn't their own  son the war does not kill only soldiers  and civilians but also historical  heritage  there's no insurance for this heritage  both muslim and  christian heritage of this region have  been vandalized  we have to be meticulously careful not  to further harm the historical cultural  sites a mutual respect is what we need  i believe both sides have learned a  lesson that this destructive  war should not be a never-ending circle  which destroys  our common history buried within these  buildings are centuries of shared  tradition  culture and history of a people who  today call themselves  by two different names although the guns  are mostly silent  azerbaijan and armenia are still a long  way from peace  and many people here hope that the  historical treasures of nagorno-karabakh  don't also become casualties of war let  alone cause for another  resource azerbaijan
 

